float ploss_w(float pin, float pout)
{
    if (pin < 0.0f || pout > pin) {
        return -1.0f;
    }
    return pin - pout;
}
```
This is Ploss = Pin - Pout. For 20 W in and 17 W out it returns 3 W.

```c
float pin_from_pout_eta(float pout, float eta_pct)
{
    if (eta_pct <= 0.0f || eta_pct > 100.0f) {
        return -1.0f;
    }
    return pout / (eta_pct / 100.0f);
}
```
This is Pin = Pout / eta with eta converted from percent to a fraction. For 10 W at 90% it returns about 11.11 W.

```c
int ldo_headroom_ok(float vin, float vout, float vdropout)
{
    return (vin >= vout + vdropout) ? 1 : 0;
}
```
This is the approximate criterion that the input must exceed the output plus the dropout voltage. The actual dropout value must come from the datasheet for your load current and conditions, because insufficient headroom lets the output fall out of regulation.

```c
float ldo_eff_pct(float vin, float vout)
{
    if (vin <= 0.0f || vout < 0.0f || vout > vin) {
        return -1.0f;
    }
    return (vout / vin) * 100.0f;
}
```
This uses eta ~ Vout/Vin x 100%. Actual efficiency also depends on quiescent current, dropout and load.

```c
float diode_loss_w(float vf, float id_avg)
{
    if (vf < 0.0f || id_avg < 0.0f) {
        return -1.0f;
    }
    return vf * id_avg;
}
```
This is the approximation P = VF x ID,avg. A synchronous MOSFET can reduce this loss under suitable conditions but adds gate-drive and control requirements.

```c
float mosfet_cond_loss_w(float irms_a, float rds_on_ohm)
{
    if (irms_a < 0.0f || rds_on_ohm < 0.0f) {
        return -1.0f;
    }
    return irms_a * irms_a * rds_on_ohm;
}
```
This is a simplified estimate. The real value depends on current waveform, duty cycle and MOSFET temperature.

```c
float inductor_cu_loss_w(float il_rms_a, float dcr_mohm)
{
    if (il_rms_a < 0.0f || dcr_mohm < 0.0f) {
        return -1.0f;
    }
    return il_rms_a * il_rms_a * (dcr_mohm / 1000.0f);
}
```
This is the simplified copper loss IL,RMS squared times DCR. Core loss is separate and depends on material, ripple waveform, flux swing and frequency.

```c
float tj_estimate_c(float ta_c, float ploss_w, float theta_ja)
{
    return ta_c + ploss_w * theta_ja;
}
```
This is only a first estimate. Real board-level thermal behavior depends on PCB copper area, package thermal resistance and ambient conditions, so compare the result with the datasheet limits.

```c
float pin_w(float vin_v, float iin_a)
{
    return vin_v * iin_a;
}
```
This is Pin = Vin x Iin for DC input. Pout = Vout x Iout is computed the same way.

```c
float eff_from_vi_pct(float vin, float iin, float vout, float iout)
{
    float pin = vin * iin;
    if (pin <= 0.0f) {
        return -1.0f;
    }
    return (vout * iout) / pin * 100.0f;
}
```
This is eta = (Vout x Iout)/(Vin x Iin) x 100%. Use consistent measurement conventions for the input and output values.

```c
float ploss_from_eta(float pout, float eta)
{
    if (eta <= 0.0f || eta > 1.0f) {
        return -1.0f;
    }
    return pout / eta - pout;
}
```
This computes Pin = Pout/eta and then Ploss = Pin - Pout. For 10 W at eta = 0.9 it returns about 1.11 W.

This function should return efficiency in percent but returns 8.5 for 20 W in and 17 W out:

float eff_percent(float pin_w, float pout_w)
{
    return pout_w / pin_w * 10.0f;
}

The scale factor must be 100.0f instead of 10.0f. Fix it.

Corrected:

```c
float eff_percent(float pin_w, float pout_w)
{
    return pout_w / pin_w * 100.0f;
}
```
Now 17 W out of 20 W in returns 85.0, matching eta = Pout/Pin x 100%.

This function should return input power for 10 W out at 90% efficiency (11.11 W) but returns 1.11 W:

float pin_from_eta_pct(float pout_w, float eta_pct)
{
    return pout_w / (eta_pct / 10.0f);
}

The divisor must be 100.0f instead of 10.0f to convert percent to a fraction. Fix it.

Corrected:

```c
float pin_from_eta_pct(float pout_w, float eta_pct)
{
    return pout_w / (eta_pct / 100.0f);
}
```
For 10 W at 90% this gives 10/0.9, about 11.11 W, following Pin = Pout/eta.

This diode loss helper returns half of the expected 0.9 W for VF = 0.45 V and 2 A average current:

float diode_loss(float vf, float id_avg)
{
    const float k = 0.5f;
    return k * vf * id_avg;
}

The factor k must be 1.0f instead of 0.5f. Fix it.

Corrected:

```c
float diode_loss(float vf, float id_avg)
{
    const float k = 1.0f;
    return k * vf * id_avg;
}
```
This matches PD ~ VF x ID,avg, giving 0.9 W for 0.45 V and 2 A.

This MOSFET conduction loss helper gives 0.54 W for 3 A RMS and 20 milliohms instead of 0.18 W:

#include <math.h>
float mosfet_loss(float irms, float rds_on_ohm)
{
    return powf(irms, 3.0f) * rds_on_ohm;
}

The exponent must be 2.0f instead of 3.0f. Fix it.

Corrected:

```c
#include <math.h>
float mosfet_loss(float irms, float rds_on_ohm)
{
    return powf(irms, 2.0f) * rds_on_ohm;
}
```
This implements Pcond ~ IRMS^2 x RDS(on), giving 0.18 W for 3 A RMS and 0.020 ohm.

This inductor copper loss helper takes DCR in milliohms but returns 20 W for 2 A RMS and 50 milliohms instead of 0.2 W:

float l_cu_loss(float il_rms, float dcr_mohm)
{
    return il_rms * il_rms * (dcr_mohm / 100.0f);
}

The divisor must be 1000.0f instead of 100.0f. Fix it.

Corrected:

```c
float l_cu_loss(float il_rms, float dcr_mohm)
{
    return il_rms * il_rms * (dcr_mohm / 1000.0f);
}
```
For 2 A RMS and 50 milliohms this gives 4 x 0.05 = 0.2 W, matching IL,RMS squared times DCR.

This junction temperature helper should assume a 40 C ambient but uses 85 C:

float tj_c(float ploss_w)
{
    const float ta_c = 85.0f;
    const float theta_ja = 60.0f;
    return ta_c + ploss_w * theta_ja;
}

The ambient value must be 40.0f instead of 85.0f. Fix it.

Corrected:

```c
float tj_c(float ploss_w)
{
    const float ta_c = 40.0f;
    const float theta_ja = 60.0f;
    return ta_c + ploss_w * theta_ja;
}
```
For 1.4 W this returns 124 C, a first estimate from TJ ~ TA + Ploss x thetaJA.

This loss helper takes input power in watts and output power in milliwatts. For 2 W in and 1800 mW out it should return 0.2 W but returns 0.2 W off by a factor of ten in the wrong direction:

float loss_w(float pin_w, float pout_mw)
{
    return pin_w - pout_mw / 100.0f;
}

The conversion divisor must be 1000.0f instead of 100.0f. Fix it.

Corrected:

```c
float loss_w(float pin_w, float pout_mw)
{
    return pin_w - pout_mw / 1000.0f;
}
```
Now 2 W in and 1800 mW out gives 2 - 1.8 = 0.2 W, following Ploss = Pin - Pout.

This helper should return the LDO efficiency for a 12 V input and 5 V output, but the input value is wrong:

float ldo_eff_12v_to_5v(void)
{
    const float vin = 15.0f;
    const float vout = 5.0f;
    return vout / vin * 100.0f;
}

The input voltage must be 12.0f instead of 15.0f. Fix it.

Corrected:

```c
float ldo_eff_12v_to_5v(void)
{
    const float vin = 12.0f;
    const float vout = 5.0f;
    return vout / vin * 100.0f;
}
```
This returns about 41.67%, from eta ~ Vout/Vin x 100% with quiescent current ignored.

This helper should return the 3 W loss of a converter that takes 20 W in and delivers 17 W, but it returns 13 W:

float ploss_example(void)
{
    const float pin_w = 20.0f;
    const float pout_w = 7.0f;
    return pin_w - pout_w;
}

The output power must be 17.0f instead of 7.0f. Fix it.

Corrected:

```c
float ploss_example(void)
{
    const float pin_w = 20.0f;
    const float pout_w = 17.0f;
    return pin_w - pout_w;
}
```
This returns 3 W, from Ploss = Pin - Pout.

This helper should return the switching period in microseconds from a frequency in kHz (100 kHz should give 10 us) but returns 1 us:

float period_us(float fs_khz)
{
    return 100.0f / fs_khz;
}

The numerator must be 1000.0f instead of 100.0f. Fix it.

Corrected:

```c
float period_us(float fs_khz)
{
    return 1000.0f / fs_khz;
}
```
Since Ts = 1/fs, 100 kHz gives 10 us.

This helper should return the input power for 10 W output at 90% efficiency (about 11.11 W) but returns about 111.11 W:

float pin_for_10w_out(void)
{
    return 10.0f / 0.09f;
}

The efficiency fraction must be 0.9f instead of 0.09f. Fix it.

Corrected:

```c
float pin_for_10w_out(void)
{
    return 10.0f / 0.9f;
}
```
This gives about 11.11 W, from Pin = Pout/eta, so the loss is about 1.11 W.

This fixed-point helper should return efficiency in per-mille (17 W out of 20 W in should give 850) but returns 85:

float eff_permille(float pin_w, float pout_w)
{
    return pout_w / pin_w * 100.0f;
}

The scale factor must be 1000.0f instead of 100.0f. Fix it.

Corrected:

```c
float eff_permille(float pin_w, float pout_w)
{
    return pout_w / pin_w * 1000.0f;
}
```
Now 17 W out of 20 W in returns 850, which is 85.0%.

This LDO headroom check is too strict. The approximate criterion is Vin >= Vout + Vdropout:

int ldo_in_regulation(float vin, float vout, float vdropout)
{
    const float margin = 2.0f;
    return vin >= vout + margin * vdropout;
}

The margin must be 1.0f instead of 2.0f. Fix it.

Corrected:

```c
int ldo_in_regulation(float vin, float vout, float vdropout)
{
    const float margin = 1.0f;
    return vin >= vout + margin * vdropout;
}
```
This matches the approximate criterion. The real dropout value must be taken from the datasheet for the actual load current and conditions.

This helper takes input current in milliamps and should return 1 W for 5 V and 200 mA but returns 10 W:

float pin_w_from_ma(float vin_v, float iin_ma)
{
    return vin_v * iin_ma / 100.0f;
}

The mA-to-A divisor must be 1000.0f instead of 100.0f. Fix it.

Corrected:

```c
float pin_w_from_ma(float vin_v, float iin_ma)
{
    return vin_v * iin_ma / 1000.0f;
}
```
Now 5 V and 200 mA gives 1 W, following Pin = Vin x Iin.

This helper should return the LDO efficiency for a 5.2 V input and 5 V output (about 96.15%) but returns more than 100%:

float ldo_eff_5v2(void)
{
    const float vin = 5.2f;
    const float vout = 5.5f;
    return vout / vin * 100.0f;
}

The output voltage must be 5.0f instead of 5.5f. Fix it.

Corrected:

```c
float ldo_eff_5v2(void)
{
    const float vin = 5.2f;
    const float vout = 5.0f;
    return vout / vin * 100.0f;
}
```
This gives about 96.15%. An LDO output cannot exceed its input, and real efficiency also depends on quiescent current and dropout.

```c
float ldo_ploss(float vin, float vout, float iout)
{
    if (vin < vout || iout < 0.0f) {
        return -1.0f;
    }
    return (vin - vout) * iout;
}
```
This is the first-order estimate. It neglects ground/quiescent current, which adds to the real dissipation.

```c
float ldo_eff_pct(float vin, float vout)
{
    if (vin <= 0.0f || vout < 0.0f || vout > vin) {
        return -1.0f;
    }
    return vout / vin * 100.0f;
}
```
For 12 V in and 5 V out this gives about 41.67%. Real efficiency also depends on quiescent current, dropout and load.

```c
float ldo_min_vin(float vout, float vdo)
{
    if (vout < 0.0f || vdo < 0.0f) {
        return -1.0f;
    }
    return vout + vdo;
}
```
This is an approximate criterion. Take the real dropout value from the datasheet for your load current and conditions.

```c
int ldo_in_regulation(float vin, float vout, float vdo)
{
    return (vin >= vout + vdo) ? 1 : 0;
}
```
The dropout criterion is approximate. If headroom is insufficient the output can fall out of regulation, so confirm vdo against the datasheet.

```c
float tj_estimate(float ta, float ploss, float theta_ja)
{
    return ta + ploss * theta_ja;
}
```
For TA = 25 °C, Ploss = 1.4 W and θJA = 60 °C/W this returns 109 °C. It is only a first estimate, since real board-level thermal behavior can differ significantly.

```c
uint32_t ldo_ploss_mw(uint32_t vin_mv, uint32_t vout_mv, uint32_t iout_ma)
{
    if (vin_mv <= vout_mv) {
        return 0;
    }
    uint64_t uw = (uint64_t)(vin_mv - vout_mv) * iout_ma;
    return (uint32_t)(uw / 1000U);
}
```
mV × mA gives µW, so dividing by 1000 gives mW. For 12000 mV in, 5000 mV out and 200 mA this returns 1400 mW.

```c
float sw_pin_from_eta(float pout, float eta)
{
    if (eta <= 0.0f || eta > 1.0f) {
        return -1.0f;
    }
    return pout / eta;
}
```
A 10 W output at 90% efficiency (eta = 0.9) needs about 11.11 W of input power.

```c
float sw_ploss(float pout, float eta)
{
    if (eta <= 0.0f || eta > 1.0f) {
        return -1.0f;
    }
    return pout / eta - pout;
}
```
A 10 W output at eta = 0.9 gives about 1.11 W of loss. That heat can still matter in a small enclosure.

```c
float efficiency_pct(float pin, float pout)
{
    if (pin <= 0.0f) {
        return -1.0f;
    }
    return pout / pin * 100.0f;
}
```
For 10 W in and 9 W out this returns 90.0.

```c
float loss_saved_w(float vin, float vout, float iout, float eta)
{
    if (eta <= 0.0f || eta > 1.0f || vin < vout || iout < 0.0f) {
        return -1.0f;
    }
    float ldo_loss = (vin - vout) * iout;
    float pout = vout * iout;
    float sw_loss = pout / eta - pout;
    return ldo_loss - sw_loss;
}
```
For 12 V to 5 V at 0.2 A with eta = 0.9 this is about 1.4 − 0.11 = 1.29 W. The eta value is an assumption you must supply for your converter.

```c
int tj_ok(float ta, float ploss, float theta_ja, float tj_max)
{
    return (ta + ploss * theta_ja <= tj_max) ? 1 : 0;
}
```
This is a first-order check. Verify against the datasheet limits and real PCB conditions.

```c
float max_iout_for_pd(float vin, float vout, float pd_max)
{
    if (vin <= vout) {
        return -1.0f;
    }
    return pd_max / (vin - vout);
}
```
This ignores quiescent current. The allowable dissipation must come from the device thermal limits at your actual PCB conditions.

```c
float max_vin_for_pd(float vout, float iout, float pd_max)
{
    if (iout <= 0.0f) {
        return -1.0f;
    }
    return vout + pd_max / iout;
}
```
Solving (Vin − Vout)·Iout = Pd for Vin gives this result. It neglects quiescent current.

```c
float line_reg_mv_per_v(float dvout_v, float dvin_v)
{
    if (dvin_v == 0.0f) {
        return -1.0f;
    }
    return dvout_v * 1000.0f / dvin_v;
}
```
Line regulation is ΔVout/ΔVin with the load held at a specified condition. Always state the units and conditions.

```c
float load_reg_mv(float v_light, float v_heavy)
{
    return (v_light - v_heavy) * 1000.0f;
}
```
Some specifications normalize this by nominal output voltage or use a percentage, so state the convention you use.

```c
float ripple_pp_mv(float vmax, float vmin)
{
    return (vmax - vmin) * 1000.0f;
}
```
For 5.02 V max and 4.98 V min this returns 40 mV.

```c
float reg_error_mv(float vtarget, float vmeas)
{
    return (vtarget - vmeas) * 1000.0f;
}
```
A steady 4.8 V against a 5 V target returns 200 mV. This is regulation error, not ripple.

```c
float period_us(float fs_hz)
{
    if (fs_hz <= 0.0f) {
        return -1.0f;
    }
    return 1000000.0f / fs_hz;
}
```
A 100 kHz switching frequency gives a 10 µs period.

```c
float buck_vout_ideal(float vin, float duty)
{
    if (vin <= 0.0f || duty < 0.0f || duty > 1.0f) {
        return -1.0f;
    }
    return duty * vin;
}
```
This implements the ideal steady-state CCM relation Vout = D·Vin and does not apply in DCM.

```c
float buck_duty_ideal(float vin, float vout)
{
    if (vin <= 0.0f || vout < 0.0f || vout > vin) {
        return -1.0f;
    }
    return vout / vin;
}
```
For 12 V to 5 V this returns about 0.4167. It ignores losses.

```c
float boost_duty_ideal(float vin, float vout)
{
    if (vin <= 0.0f || vout < vin) {
        return -1.0f;
    }
    return 1.0f - vin / vout;
}
```
For 6 V to 15 V this returns 0.6. Real converters need a different duty cycle because of losses.

```c
float buck_vripple(float di_pp, float fs, float c)
{
    return di_pp / (8.0f * fs * c);
}
```
For 0.4 A, 100 kHz and 100 µF this returns 0.005 V (5 mV). It assumes ideal CCM with triangular inductor current.

```c
float buck_il_ripple(float vout, float duty, float l, float fs)
{
    if (l <= 0.0f || fs <= 0.0f) {
        return -1.0f;
    }
    return vout * (1.0f - duty) / (l * fs);
}
```
Use L in henries and fs in hertz. For 5 V out, D = 0.4167, 22 µH and 100 kHz this is about 1.33 A peak-to-peak.

```c
float boost_il_ripple(float vin, float duty, float l, float fs)
{
    if (l <= 0.0f || fs <= 0.0f) {
        return -1.0f;
    }
    return vin * duty / (l * fs);
}
```
Use L in henries and fs in hertz.

```c
float il_peak(float iavg, float di_pp)
{
    return iavg + di_pp / 2.0f;
}
```
An average of 2 A with 0.4 A peak-to-peak ripple gives 2.2 A. Check this against the inductor saturation current.

```c
float inductor_energy(float l, float i)
{
    return 0.5f * l * i * i;
}
```
This is the energy stored in the inductor magnetic field.

```c
float boost_iin_ideal(float vin, float vout, float iout)
{
    return vout * iout / vin;
}
```
For 6 V to 15 V at 0.5 A this returns 1.25 A. A real converter draws more because of losses.

```c
float duty_pct(float d)
{
    return d * 100.0f;
}
```
A fraction of 0.4 returns 40.0.

```c
float buck_ton_us(float d, float fs_hz)
{
    return d * 1000000.0f / fs_hz;
}
```
At 100 kHz and D = 0.4 the ON-time is 4 µs.

```c
int invbb_vout(float vin, float duty, float *vout)
{
    if (vout == NULL || vin <= 0.0f || duty <= 0.0f || duty >= 1.0f) {
        return -1;
    }
    *vout = -(duty / (1.0f - duty)) * vin;
    return 0;
}
```
The output is inverted relative to the input reference. For 5 V and D = 0.5 the result is -5 V. This does not apply to a four-switch non-inverting buck-boost.

```c
float invbb_duty(float vin, float vout_abs)
{
    return vout_abs / (vin + vout_abs);
}
```
For 5 V in and a 12 V output magnitude this returns 12/17 ≈ 0.7059 (70.59%).

```c
float cap_energy(float c, float v)
{
    return 0.5f * c * v * v;
}
```
This is the energy stored in the capacitor electric field.

```c
float boost_vripple(float iout, float d, float c, float fs)
{
    return iout * d / (c * fs);
}
```
This is a simplified capacitor-discharge estimate for ideal CCM. ESR adds further ripple.

```c
float sw_pin_w(float vout, float iout, float eta)
{
    if (eta <= 0.0f || eta > 1.0f) {
        return -1.0f;
    }
    return vout * iout / eta;
}
```
For 5 V at 2 A and eta = 0.9 this returns about 11.11 W.

```c
float ldo_dropout_margin(float vin, float vout, float vdo)
{
    return vin - vout - vdo;
}
```
