        return 0U;
    }
    return 1000000000UL / fs_hz;
}
```
For fs = 20,000 Hz this returns 50,000 ns, which is 50 us.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

float alias_hz(float f_in_hz, float fs_hz);

void print_alias_table(void)
{
    static const float inputs_hz[] = { 70.0f, 160.0f, 140.0f };
    const float fs_hz = 100.0f;
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
        printf("%.0f Hz -> %.0f Hz\n", inputs_hz[i], alias_hz(inputs_hz[i], fs_hz));
    }
}
```
The expected output is 70 -> 30, 160 -> 40 and 140 -> 40.

```cpp
constexpr double sample_period_s(double fs_hz) { return 1.0 / fs_hz; }
constexpr double nyquist_hz(double fs_hz) { return fs_hz / 2.0; }
```
These follow Ts = 1/fs and fN = fs/2. Callers must pass a nonzero fs_hz.

```c
#define MIN_FS_HZ 6000U

static inline int fs_ok(unsigned fs_hz)
{
    return fs_hz > MIN_FS_HZ;
}
```
The ideal criterion is fs > 2*3 kHz = 6 kHz. A practical design should add margin.

```cpp
class SamplingConfig {
public:
    explicit SamplingConfig(double fs_hz) : fs_hz_(fs_hz) {}
    double period_s() const { return 1.0 / fs_hz_; }
    double nyquist_hz() const { return fs_hz_ / 2.0; }
private:
    double fs_hz_;
};
```
For fs = 20 kHz, period_s() is 50 us and nyquist_hz() is 10 kHz. The constructor does not guard against fs_hz = 0.

```c
#include <stdint.h>

uint32_t sample_count(uint32_t fs_hz, uint32_t duration_ms)
{
    return (fs_hz * duration_ms) / 1000UL;
}
```
At 20,000 samples per second, a 100 ms window gives 2000 samples. The product fs_hz * duration_ms can overflow uint32_t for large values.

```c
#include <stdint.h>

double sample_time_us(uint32_t n, double fs_hz)
{
    return ((double)n / fs_hz) * 1000000.0;
}
```
Here Ts = 1/fs, so t = n/fs seconds. At fs = 20 kHz, sample n = 4 occurs at 200 us. fs_hz must be nonzero.

```c
int validate_sampling(float fs_hz, float f_max_hz)
{
    if (f_max_hz <= 0.0f) {
        return -1;
    }
    if (fs_hz > 2.0f * f_max_hz) {
        return 0;
    }
    return -2;
}
```
A return of 0 means only that the ideal criterion is met. Margin and analog anti-alias filtering still need to be checked separately.

```c
#include <stdint.h>

uint32_t min_fs_integer_hz(uint32_t f_max_hz)
{
    return 2U * f_max_hz + 1U;
}
```
For f_max = 3000 Hz it returns 6001 Hz. This satisfies only the ideal criterion fs > 2*fmax. Real systems need additional margin.

```c
int count_above_nyquist(const float *f_hz, int n, float fs_hz)
{
    int count = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (f_hz[i] > fs_hz / 2.0f) {
            count++;
        }
    }
    return count;
}
```
Each counted frequency lies above fN = fs/2 and can alias into the lower band.

```c
#include <math.h>

float alias_freq(float f_in, float fs)
{
    float k = roundf(f_in / fs);
    return fabsf(f_in - k * fs);
}
```
roundf selects the integer k nearest to f_in/fs, so the result lies between 0 and fs/2. Example: alias_freq(160.0f, 100.0f) returns 40.0f.

```c
#include <stdbool.h>

bool nyquist_ok(float fs, float f_max)
{
    return fs > 2.0f * f_max;
}
```
The comparison is strict because the boundary case fs = 2·f_max needs particular care. Real designs also need margin for the anti-aliasing filter.

```c
float rc_cutoff_hz(float r_ohm, float c_farad)
{
    const float two_pi = 6.2831853f;
    return 1.0f / (two_pi * r_ohm * c_farad);
}
```
Example: rc_cutoff_hz(10000.0f, 10e-9f) returns about 1591.5 Hz. This applies to an ideal, unloaded filter.

```c
#include <stdint.h>

double adc_lsb_volts(double v_min, double v_max, uint8_t bits)
{
    double codes = (double)(1UL << bits);
    return (v_max - v_min) / codes;
}
```
This computes the nominal ideal LSB as (Vmax - Vmin) / 2^N. A 12-bit ADC over 0 V to 3.3 V gives about 0.806 mV. This is nominal code spacing, not a guarantee of absolute accuracy.

```c
double adc_max_quant_error_mv(double lsb_mv)
{
    return lsb_mv / 2.0;
}
```
For an ideal nearest-level quantizer the error is bounded by plus or minus half an LSB. A 2 mV LSB therefore gives a maximum error magnitude of 1 mV. Other ADC errors such as noise or offset are not included.

```c
#include <math.h>

double adc_quant_noise_rms_mv(double lsb_mv)
{
    return lsb_mv / sqrt(12.0);
}
```
This assumes quantization error is uniformly distributed over plus or minus half an LSB and uncorrelated with the signal. A 2 mV LSB gives about 0.577 mV RMS. The model may not hold for very small, constant or correlated inputs.

```c
#include <stdint.h>

double adc_ideal_snr_db(uint8_t bits)
{
    return 6.02 * bits + 1.76;
}
```
For 12 bits this returns about 74.0 dB. The result applies only to an ideal converter with a full-scale sine input. Real noise, distortion, clock jitter and analog circuitry lower the measured SNR.

```c
double adc_enob_from_sinad(double sinad_db)
{
    return (sinad_db - 1.76) / 6.02;
}
```
A measured SINAD of 62 dB gives about 10.0 bits. ENOB depends on test conditions such as input frequency, sample rate, noise and distortion, so it is not the same as nominal resolution.

```c
#include <stdint.h>

uint32_t adc_code_count(uint8_t bits)
{
    return 1UL << bits;
}
```
An N-bit ADC has 2^N possible output codes. For example, 12 bits gives 4,096 codes and 16 bits gives 65,536 codes.

This LSB calculation for a 10-bit ADC over 0 V to 5 V is wrong:

double lsb_mv = 5000.0 / 512.0;

The divisor must be 1024.0 (2^10 codes). Correct it.

This ENOB function has a wrong constant:

double enob = (sinad_db - 1.76) / 6.20;

The divisor must be 6.02. Provide the corrected line.

This ideal-SNR line has a wrong offset constant:

double snr_db = 6.02 * bits + 1.67;

The constant must be 1.76. Correct it.
